# Language rules (from Language Phase 0, 14 October 2026)

Why: HatchGrab will need US English now and other languages later. Adding a language must be adding a
translation file, never another sweep. Source: `docs/language-readiness-audit-report.md` §4.5 and
`docs/language-phase-0-report.md`.

**Words that work in both countries are preferred in new copy; existing UK text stays byte-identical until its phase.**

1. **No new user-facing text written inline.** New words go in `messages/en-GB.json` and are read with `useT`
   (client components, `lib/i18n/client.tsx`), `getTranslations` (server components) or `translatorFor` (emails,
   PDFs, API responses, tickets, crons — `lib/i18n/translator.ts`). `scripts/ui-literals.cjs` fails if a new file
   carries JSX text or a literal placeholder, title, label, aria-label or alt. US wording goes in
   `messages/en-US.json` only when it differs.
   - **A file that reads a namespace imports its catalog module** — `import '@/lib/i18n/catalog/<namespace>'` beside
     `useT` (from `@/lib/i18n/use-t`) or `translatorFor`. The browser then carries only the namespaces its files read
     (Language Phase 1b). The per-namespace files (`messages/ns/**`, `lib/i18n/catalog/*`) are GENERATED from the two
     authored files by `node scripts/split-messages.cjs` (run by `npm run build` / `npm run dev`); never edit them by
     hand. `scripts/messages-split.cjs` fails if one is stale or a file reads a namespace it does not import
     (`--fix` adds the import).
   - **A refusal a route sends is `{ error, …, code, params }`** — `customerErrorBody` (customers),
     `kitchenErrorBody` (the kitchen and operator) or `onboardingErrorBody` (setup, signup, auth, demo); a screen
     shows `errorText(t, body, fallback)`. All three are thin wrappers over ONE helper, `errorBody(namespace, …)` in
     `lib/i18n/error-body.ts` (Language Phase 1c): a new surface adds a wrapper for its `errors…` namespace, never a
     copy of the body-building code.
2. **Whole sentences only.** Never build a sentence from pieces (`'Only ' + n + ' left'`, a prefix and a suffix
   around a control, a bold word split out). One message with placeholders, and rich-text tags for bold or links.
3. **No hand-written plurals.** No `n === 1 ? … : …` and no added `'s'`. Use ICU `plural` in the message.
4. **No case changes on text.** No `toUpperCase` / `toLowerCase` / capitalising on translatable or user text. CSS
   `uppercase` only on short labels, and never on the truck's own data (category names, plan ids).
5. **Dates, times, numbers and money only through `lib/time-utils.ts` and `lib/money-format.ts`** with the truck's
   settings, passed into messages as ready-made strings. No `'en-GB'` literal, no month or day lists, no ordinals
   by hand, no `toFixed` for display, no currency symbol joined by hand.
   - Weekday and month names come from Intl (`weekdayName`, `monthName`); ordinals from `ordinalDay`.
   - ⚠️ **The short month is a rule for English only:** the first three letters of Intl's full month name
     ("September" → "Sep"). Three-letter truncation fails in other languages (French "juin" and "juillet" are both
     "jui"), so **each later language must define its short month form when it is added** (`SHORT_MONTH_RULES` in
     `lib/time-utils.ts`).
6. **Display text is never a key.** Compare ids, codes or enum values, never words ('Walk-up', 'Coming soon', a
   plan row's name, a formatted price). Plan rows have `id`s, price words are `PRICE_WORD`, contact topics are
   `contactHref(id)`, walk-ups are `isWalkUp()`.
7. **Servers send codes and values; screens supply the words.** New API errors return `{ code, params }` with an
   English fallback. Never put a sentence in a URL.
8. **Store codes, not sentences.** No new English sentence in a database row that a customer later sees.
9. **Leave room for longer text.** No new fixed pixel widths, `nowrap` or `truncate` on button labels or headings
   without checking the text at 130% of its English length (German runs about 30% longer).
10. **AI prompts state the output language** and keep the menu's own language. New prompts take the truck's
    language as a parameter.
11. **Words that work in both countries are preferred in new copy** where they read naturally ("truck" not
    "van"; "pickup" where the UK text would otherwise be new). UK text that already exists stays byte-identical
    until its phase.

Who sees which language: a truck's screens, tickets, kitchen and emails, and its customers, use its country's
`country_settings.locale` (en-GB / en-US), else en-GB. Marketing pages use the visitor's resolved country. Only
en-GB and en-US are valid today (`lib/i18n/locales.ts`). "Pay at Hatch" is a brand name in every country.
